The InfinityGrid headline:

MCGRAW HILL: THE ATTACKER MAY NOT HAVE NEEDED AN ACCOUNT

And the technical subhead:

A public-facing SaaS page is still part of your security perimeter when it can reach enterprise data.

That is the part worth drilling into.



Executive Technical Summary

McGraw Hill identified unauthorized access to data available through a Salesforce-hosted webpage. The company stated that the activity appeared to be associated with a broader configuration issue affecting Salesforce customers and emphasized that the incident did not represent unauthorized access to McGraw Hill's Salesforce accounts, customer databases, courseware, or internal systems.

The distinction is technically important.

The public evidence currently supports:

PUBLIC / EXTERNALLY REACHABLE WEB EXPERIENCE
                ↓
IMPROPERLY EXPOSED DATA
                ↓
UNAUTHORIZED RETRIEVAL
                ↓
EXTORTION
                ↓
PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION

It does not currently establish:

COMPROMISED EMPLOYEE
↓
STOLEN PASSWORD
↓
SALESFORCE LOGIN
↓
ADMIN TAKEOVER

That makes this fundamentally different from identity-driven Salesforce breaches.

The underlying security problem appears to concern what an external user could access without compromising a conventional employee identity. McGraw Hill tied its incident broadly to a Salesforce configuration issue, while Salesforce separately documented an active campaign exploiting overly permissive Experience Cloud guest configurations.

